  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nThe wavy west-to-east quasi-stationary front referenced in \nprevious discussions still remains draped over the Central \nPlains into the mid-Mississippi River Valley and southern Ohio \nValley regions. Weak high pressure sits centered over the Great \nLakes, spread over the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest. \nScattered showers with a few weak embedded thunderstorms will \ncontinue to drift southeast from eastern SD through \nsouthwestern-southern MN into northern IA through daybreak, \nalthough with no severe weather expected as the dynamics and \nbest instability will sit well south of the WFO MPX coverage \narea. \n\nA relative break in the action is expected from this afternoon\ninto tonight, then another round of showers with possibly a \nfew thunderstorms will develop over far southern MN on Friday \nthen slowly spread northward Friday night through Saturday. The \naforementioned front to the south will kink northward into the \nDakotas Friday into Saturday in response to an upper level \ndisturbance rushing across the area within a more zonal flow \naloft. This will commence weak cyclogenesis over central ND on \nSaturday, with that newly-formed low pressing east into the \nGreat Lakes by Saturday evening. QPF continues to drop with this\nFriday-Saturday spread of rainfall, so although the rainfall is\ngreatly welcome across the area, this will not be anything to \nput significant dents into the ongoing drought over much of \nMN/WI. \n\nA decent drying out period is expected from late Saturday night \nthrough late Monday with flattened ridging moving across the \narea aloft underneath surface high pressure. Come Monday night, \nanother west-to-east frontal boundary looks to drop south from \ncentral Canada into the northern tier CONUS while zonal flow \nresumes. This will bring Pacific moisture across the Rockies \ninto the Upper Midwest to interact with the newly-arriving \nfront, putting chances for rainfall daily into the forecast \nstarting Monday night and continuing through midweek. No strong \nstorms or heavy rain is forecast for any given 12-hr period, \njust occasional rain showers here and there for each day.\n\nTemperatures will drop back to near normal levels starting today \nwith more zonal flow aloft developing and cooler Canadian air \ntaking up more of a residence over the northern tier states. \nWith no appreciable airmass change coming through at least the \nmiddle of next week, temperatures are expected to remain within \na couple degrees of normal values throughout this forecast. \nShould any days feature more rainfall than not, temperatures \ncould be held a few more degrees below normal than currently \nforecast.",
